Team,

Annex distilling Calthorpe Part 8 (5 June) plus the Henry Calder operator
call (4 June) plus the Tasman Mercantile follow-up email (excerpt below)
into a single page. Use this for two purposes:

  1. The T4 briefing video should open with a short business-model
     primer before the findings / open-questions sections - what an
     egg producer actually is, why the unit economics work the way
     they do, and what optionality the asset does and does not carry.

  2. The T3 deck should reflect the alternative-use optionality
     question as an open item in confirmatory diligence, not as a
     standalone bottom-line opportunity, and the manure / by-product
     revenue offset belongs in the bottom-line opportunities section.

Verbatim figures triangulated from Calthorpe Part 8, Calder transcript,
Bucket-4 supplier exposure annex (3 June, manure offtake offset),
and public sources: NZ MPI Layer Hens Code of Welfare, Hy-Line / Lohmann
performance guides, Lodestone Energy public site, AWS / Microsoft / CDC
NZ public announcements, NPS Highly Productive Land 2022.
Tables come through as screenshots out of my working annex - ask me
if you want the underlying file.

- Iris

1. production-cycle primer (for T4 video primer + T3 asset profile)

Three phases. All three are on the same farm at any one time
because flocks are staggered.

Source: Calder [00:01:18]+[00:03:12]+[00:05:48]; Hy-Line / Lohmann
Brown commercial performance guides; NZ MPI Layer Hens code-of-welfare
production benchmarks (~280 eggs / hen / year industry standard).

Implication for Aurora's 610k bird flock - the operating reality is a
~720-800k total population at any one time once you include the
parallel pullet population (~120k). Cycle through 600k birds every
~15-18 months. The bird is a depreciating asset on an 18-month clock
(Calder [00:30:46] - the operational read).

2. spent hen + by-product economics

Three pathways for spent hens, all low-margin:

Note (Calder [00:07:42]+[00:10:48]): "Spent-hen disposal is closer to
a cost than a revenue line on a modern NZ layer farm... If a sell-side
deck is treating spent-hen disposal as a hidden value lever, I'd want
to see the offtake contract, not the research note."

Egg grading mix:

  Grade A (retail)            ~65-75% of total volume   Main money
  Grade B / cracked / liquid  ~25-30%                   Versova
                                                        (regional
                                                        breaker), low
                                                        margin
  Spoilage / waste            ~1-5%                     Negative

Source: Calder [00:15:14].

3. manure offtake - real revenue offset, often missed

NZ layer-farm manure goes to Ravensdown and Ballance Agri-Nutrients
- the two main NZ fertiliser co-ops - as a Class A organic input.
Pricing typically NZ$15-25 per tonne depending on year, moisture,
and stockpile age.

For Aurora:

  Bird population     ~610k layer flock
  Manure throughput   ~18,000-22,000 tonnes / year
  Realized revenue    NZ$300,000-500,000 / year (NZ$280k peak Calder
                      year cited as a comparable benchmark for a
                      ~600k-bird operation the size of Aurora's)

Belongs in T3 bottom-line opportunities section as a quantified
revenue offset that often gets missed in shell-egg sell-side framing
(per the Halberd analytical standards section 8: "Bottom-line / cost-
out levers are commonly under-weighted by analysts focused on
top-line stories").

Source: Calder [00:12:22]; Calthorpe Part 7 [00:51:14] (Bucket 4
supplier exposure annex, 3 June, section 9).

4. alternative-use optionality - realistic scenario set

Five pathways - across all five, the realizable buyer pool for Aurora's
two specific sites is small.

Consenting overlay (Part 8 [00:18:48]+[00:23:42]):

  - Both Aurora sites sit on rural-production-zoned land.
  - Both sit on HPL-overlay soils (NPS Highly Productive Land,
    October 2022). Most of the Canterbury Plains is LUC Class
    1-2; most of the Waikato dairy/mixed-cropping belt is LUC
    1-3.
  - Consenting timeline 24-36 months scoping-to-determination,
    plus submitter and Environment Court appeal rights.
  - Practical effect: a buyer absorbing the consenting pathway
    is paying for option value, not realised value.

5. aurora-site optionality read - part 8 [00:23:42]+[00:27:18] verbatim

  "Aurora has two sites. North Island - Waikato, south of Auckland.
  South Island - Canterbury Plains near Christchurch, the Selwyn-
  catchment site Henry mentioned. Both sit on rural-production-zoned
  land. Both sit on HPL-overlay soils. Neither is in the north-west
  Auckland data-centre cluster... So the realistic alternative-use
  optionality on Aurora's two specific sites is - small. Neighbour
  amalgamation at rural-production going-concern. That's the floor
  and that's roughly the ceiling. The deck should not carry an
  alternative-use optionality premium on Aurora's headline
  valuation. If Tasman Mercantile is floating one in their
  follow-up email, it's a sell-side talking point, not a
  realisable bid."

  "I'd put the alternative-use optionality conversation in the
  deck as an open question on confirmatory diligence - not as
  an opportunity. The question is - is there a specific named
  non-egg buyer prepared to bid on either site at a premium to
  rural-production going-concern, and at what consenting risk?
  Until that buyer is named, it's an optionality talking point
  with negative expected value once you factor in consenting
  cost and time."

6. sell-side optionality framing - verbatim excerpt from Tasman
   Mercantile follow-up email (Nicholas Chen to V. Holst,
   28 May 2026, forwarded by Vivian to deal-team)

  From: Nicholas Chen <nicholas.chen@tasmanmercantile.co.nz>
  To: Vivian Holst <vivian.holst@halberd.com>
  Date: Wed, 28 May 2026 14:38:00 +1200
  Subject: Project Albatross - additional context

  "Vivian - one further point ahead of the IM. The McKenna family
  has been very thoughtful about the Aurora land bank as a
  long-term asset. Both production sites sit on substantial rural
  acreage with meaningful alternative-use optionality beyond the
  egg business - the Canterbury site in particular has been
  examined for alternative agricultural and infrastructure uses
  including data-centre and utility-scale solar applications, and
  the Waikato site likewise carries optionality on adjacent
  agricultural conversion routes. We see this as a non-trivial
  underpin on the headline transaction value irrespective of the
  shell-egg outlook. Happy to walk this through if it's of
  interest to your IC."

NB: Test this framing rigorously. Calthorpe Part 8 + Calder
operator read directly contradict the realizability of the
alternative-use optionality on either site at a buy-out premium.

7. T4 briefing-video primer + T3 deck-read text

T4 video opens with the business-model primer before the findings
+ open-questions sections (per Vivian, 6 June Slack thread - "the
senior partner needs the basics before the findings"). Suggested
primer (~12-15 seconds at briefing pacing):

  "Aurora is a New Zealand shell-egg producer. The unit economics
  run on an 18-month bird cycle - eighteen weeks of pullet sunk
  cost, fifty weeks of peak lay at roughly 300 eggs per hen per
  year, and depopulation around seventy-five weeks. The bird is a
  depreciating asset; the sheds, the brand, the customer
  relationships are downstream of getting the next pullet flock
  into peak lay on schedule. Spent-hen disposal is a low-margin
  rendering / pet-food channel - call it a cost not a revenue
  line. Manure offtake to Ravensdown and Ballance is a real
  NZ$300-500k per year revenue offset that often gets missed.
  Alternative-use optionality on the underlying landholdings is
  small at this scale - Aurora's two sites sit on rural-zoned,
  HPL-overlay land outside the Auckland data-center cluster, with
  24-36 month consenting risk on any non-rural use. With that
  context, here are our three preliminary findings, followed by
  our three open questions for confirmatory diligence."

T3 deck risk-register / open-questions section addition:

  "alternative-use optionality - sell-side broker has framed both
  Aurora sites as carrying meaningful land-bank optionality
  beyond the egg business (data-center, solar, dairy conversion).
  Independent industry expert read (Calthorpe Part 8) and
  operator read (Calder) directly contradict the realizability
  of that optionality on either of Aurora's two specific sites:
  both sit on rural-production-zoned, HPL-overlay land, both are
  outside the Auckland NW data-center cluster, with 24-36 month
  consenting risk on any non-rural use. Treat as open question on
  confirmatory diligence - is there a specific named non-egg
  buyer prepared to bid at a premium to rural-production going-
  concern - rather than as a bottom-line opportunity in the
  headline valuation."

T3 bottom-line opportunities section - manure / by-product:

  "manure offtake revenue offset - Ravensdown / Ballance
  Agri-Nutrients buy NZ layer-farm manure as a Class A organic
  input at NZ$15-25 per tonne. Aurora 610k flock implies
  18,000-22,000 tonnes / year and NZ$300-500k / year revenue
  offset. Small but commonly missed in shell-egg sell-side
  framing."

End annex.

